@database
@$VER: Host-Tools.guide 2.9 (2026-11-04)
@index Main
@wordwrap

@node Main "Host-Tools"
Host-Tools is a classic AmigaOS 68k package for Amiberry. It has command-line tools that talk to the host operating system, two optional AHI audio drivers, an MHI MP3 decoder, and this guide.

Choose a topic:

@{"Command tools" link Commands}
@{"Exit codes" link ExitCodes}
@{"UAE AHI audio driver" link AHI}
@{"UAESND AHI audio driver" link UAESND}
@{"UAE MHI MP3 decoder" link MHI}
@{"Installation notes" link InstallNotes}

@endnode

@node Commands "Command Tools"
Select "Command tools" in the installer to copy the programs to C:. They all work on Linux, macOS and Windows hosts. The only extra requirement is for host-shell on Windows, which needs Amiberry 8.6.0 or newer (see Installation Notes). Type ? after a command for usage help.

@{"host-run" link HostRun} runs a command on the host.
@{"host-multiview" link HostMultiView} opens files or URLs with the host default application.
@{"host-shell" link HostShell} opens an interactive host shell.
@{"host-path" link HostPath} prints host paths.
@{"host-reveal" link HostReveal} shows files in the host file manager.
@{"host-notify" link HostNotify} sends host desktop notifications.
@{"host-edit" link HostEdit} opens files in the host text editor.
@{"host-clip" link HostClip} copies text to and from the host clipboard.
@{"host-say" link HostSay} speaks text through the host speech synthesizer.
@{"host-info" link HostInfo} prints host integration details.
@{"host-download" link HostDownload} downloads files through the host.
@{"host-copy" link HostCopy} copies one file between a host path and the Amiga.
@{"host-env" link HostEnv} gets and sets host environment variables.
@{"host-quit" link HostQuit} closes Amiberry.

@{"Exit codes" link ExitCodes}

@endnode

@node ExitCodes "Exit Codes"
The command tools return 0 on success and 10 when an operation fails or an argument is missing or invalid. A tool that waits for a host command also returns 10 if that command exits with a nonzero status.

They return 2 when uae.resource is not available, for example outside Amiberry or with Native Code disabled.

Asking for usage help with ? returns 0, also when uae.resource is not available. An argument after ? is an error and returns 10.

@endnode

@node AHI "UAE AHI Audio Driver"
Select "UAE AHI audio driver" in the installer to copy the driver and its AudioMode.

Installed files:

Devs/AHI/uae.audio
Devs/AudioModes/UAE

The driver provides the classic UAE :16 bit HIFI Stereo++ AHI mode. It sends the mixed AHI audio to Amiberry through the classic UAE AHI v1 host interface.

UAE :16 bit HIFI Stereo++ is 16 bit stereo output with panning.

The installer does not change AHI preferences. To use the driver, select the UAE mode in AHI preferences yourself.

@endnode

@node UAESND "UAESND AHI Audio Driver"
Select "UAESND AHI audio driver" in the installer to copy the driver and its AudioMode.

Installed files:

Devs/AHI/uaesnd.audio
Devs/AudioModes/UAESND

The driver plays AHI audio through the UAESND sound board emulated by Amiberry. Each AHI channel is played by a hardware audio stream, so there is no Amiga-side mixing. Enable the UAESND sound board in the Amiberry configuration before you use these modes.

Available AHI modes:

uaesnd: Stereo is 16 bit stereo output.
uaesnd: HiFi Stereo is 32 bit stereo output.
uaesnd: 7.1 is 32 bit multichannel output.

The installer does not change AHI preferences. To use the driver, select a uaesnd mode in AHI preferences yourself.

Only one program can use the sound board at a time. A second AHI allocation of a uaesnd mode fails until the first program releases it. Recording needs a capture device on the host. If there is none, starting recording returns an error.

@endnode

@node MHI "UAE MHI MP3 Decoder"
Select "UAE MHI MP3 decoder" in the installer to copy the library.

Installed file:

Libs/MHI/mhiuae.library

The library implements the classic MHI decoder interface and forwards MP3 buffers to Amiberry through uae.resource. Amiberry decodes the stream with its host mpg123 decoder and feeds the result into the emulator audio mixer, so decoding takes no Amiga CPU time.

The library needs no AHI mode and does not configure one. It works with Paula, UAE AHI, UAESND AHI or any other AHI driver you choose. Enable Native Code execution in Amiberry for the uae.resource integration.

@endnode

@node HostRun "host-run"
host-run runs a command on the host operating system.

Usage:

host-run <command> [arguments...]

Arguments that name existing Amiga paths are translated to host paths before the command is sent to the host. A path on an existing volume or assign that does not exist is an error and returns 10. Other arguments, such as options and Windows paths like C:\Users\me\a.txt, go to the host as written. On Linux and macOS each argument is quoted for the host shell, so operators such as | and > are passed to the program as plain text. Use host-shell when you need them.

On Linux and macOS host-run checks that the command exists, starts it in the background and returns at once. If the command is not found, host-run prints "Command not found on host" and returns 10. The command's input and output are discarded.

On Windows the first argument is the program and the rest are its arguments. The program is started with PowerShell Start-Process and host-run returns without waiting for it. A console program opens in its own window. If the program cannot be started, host-run prints a message and returns 10. For .bat and .cmd files, arguments with & | < > or ^ are quoted. An argument with a double quote, a percent sign or a line break is refused for a batch file.

@endnode

@node HostMultiView "host-multiview"
host-multiview opens one or more files or URLs with the host operating system's default application.

Usage:

host-multiview <filename|URL> [filename2|URL2 ...]

An existing Amiga path is translated first, and a URL is passed through as a URL. A path that does not exist is an error. On Windows the default handler is found with PowerShell Start-Process.

@endnode

@node HostShell "host-shell"
host-shell opens an interactive terminal session on the host operating system, or runs one command in it.

Usage:

host-shell [command]

On Linux and macOS the host user's login shell is used, so shell profile files can set paths and environment variables. This applies to interactive sessions and to a command given on the host-shell command line. A single argument is run as written, so it can hold a whole command line. Several arguments are quoted and joined into one command.

Typed text is converted from ISO-8859-1 to UTF-8 and host output back, on every host. Characters that ISO-8859-1 lacks show as an ASCII stand-in or ?.

The host terminal is sized to match the Amiga console window, so full-screen host programs display correctly. To apply a new window size, resize the Amiga window and restart host-shell. Ctrl-C is sent to the host program and does not end host-shell.

On Windows, host-shell opens %ComSpec% (normally cmd.exe) in a ConPTY pseudo console and runs commands through cmd /C. There is no login shell. A single argument is run as written. Several arguments are joined and quoted, and an argument containing a double quote, a percent sign or a line break is refused. This needs Amiberry 8.6.0 or newer on Windows 10 version 1809 or later.

@endnode

@node HostPath "host-path"
host-path prints the host path of one or more existing Amiga paths.

Usage:

host-path <path> [path2 ...]

A path that does not exist, a volume that is not in the DOS list, a path longer than 255 characters or a URL prints an error and makes the exit code 10.

@endnode

@node HostReveal "host-reveal"
host-reveal shows one or more files in the host file manager.

Usage:

host-reveal <path> [path2 ...]

On macOS the file is selected in Finder, and on Windows in Explorer, started through PowerShell. On Linux the file is selected if the default file manager supports the FileManager1 desktop interface. Otherwise the containing directory is opened. URLs are refused.

@endnode

@node HostNotify "host-notify"
host-notify sends a notification through the host desktop environment.

Usage:

host-notify <message>
host-notify <title> <message...>

With one argument the title is Amiga. On Linux the notification is sent with notify-send, on macOS with osascript, and on Windows as a toast. A title or message that starts with - is shown as text. The Windows toast is shown under the name Windows PowerShell. Title and message together are limited to about 850 characters on Windows.

@endnode

@node HostEdit "host-edit"
host-edit opens files in the host desktop text editor.

Usage:

host-edit <path> [path2 ...]

On Linux the files open in the application the desktop associates with plain text, started through xdg-mime and gtk-launch. If there is none, VISUAL or EDITOR is used, and xdg-open only as a last resort. The type of the file itself is not consulted, so an image or a PDF still opens in the text editor. VISUAL and EDITOR start without a terminal, so they need a graphical editor. macOS uses open -t. On Windows the files are opened in Notepad. URLs are refused. A file that does not exist yet is created in its directory, which must exist and have a host path. If no editor can be started, host-edit prints a message and returns 10. VISUAL, EDITOR and xdg-open start in the background.

@endnode

@node HostClip "host-clip"
host-clip copies text to the host clipboard or prints the current host clipboard contents.

Usage:

host-clip [copy] <text...>
host-clip copy < file
host-clip paste

Without text arguments, host-clip copy reads standard input unchanged, including line breaks. Pasted text also keeps its trailing line breaks. Copied text is limited to 2047 characters. Input that contains a NUL byte or cannot be read is refused and the clipboard stays as it was. Text is converted between the Amiga ISO-8859-1 character set and the host character set automatically, through iconv on Linux and macOS and through PowerShell on Windows.

@endnode

@node HostSay "host-say"
host-say speaks text through the host speech synthesizer and returns when the speech ends.

Usage:

host-say <text...>
host-say < file

Without text arguments, host-say reads standard input. Typing host-say alone at a console is an error. Ctrl-C stops the speech. The text is limited to 2047 characters, or about 1000 on Windows, and may start with a dash.

macOS uses say. Linux uses spd-say, then espeak-ng, then espeak, whichever is installed first, and host-say fails if none is. Windows uses the System.Speech synthesizer through PowerShell. Text is converted from ISO-8859-1 to UTF-8 through iconv on Linux and macOS and through PowerShell on Windows.

host-say keeps waiting for as long as the host reports the speech program running, even past the 30 second limit of the other tools. On Amiberry builds without status support it gives up after about 5 seconds.

@endnode

@node HostInfo "host-info"
host-info prints the host operating system and user, and the shell, editor, opener and clipboard backend that the other tools use.

Usage:

host-info

@endnode

@node HostDownload "host-download"
host-download downloads an HTTP, HTTPS, FTP or FTPS URL on the host and saves it to an Amiga path. The host's curl or wget does the transfer, so HTTPS and modern servers work.

Usage:

host-download <URL> [<destination>] [FORCE]

Without a destination, the file is saved in the current directory under the name from the URL. A destination that is an existing directory also keeps the URL's file name. An existing destination file is overwritten only when FORCE is given. The name from the URL must be a single file name. If the end of the URL contains a colon, is . or .., or contains control characters, host-download stops and asks for a destination file. A name longer than 107 characters is shortened, with a warning.

The file is written through AmigaDOS, so any writable volume works, including RAM: and hardfiles. It is first written to a temporary file beside the destination and moved into place only after a successful transfer. A failed, stalled or aborted download leaves no partial file. With FORCE, the old destination is kept until the replacement is ready. Press Ctrl-C to abort. Downloads do not resume.

On older Linux and macOS builds the host downloads the whole file before sending it, and sends a space about once a second meanwhile, so a slow download is not taken for a stalled one. The size shown comes from a HEAD request; a server that refuses HEAD still works, with progress shown in kilobytes. Curly braces and square brackets in a URL are not expanded. On Windows the URL reaches curl.exe through PowerShell, so %NAME% in a URL stays as typed; a URL with a double quote or a control character, or longer than about 1000 characters, is refused there.

host-download works on Linux, macOS and Windows hosts. On Windows it needs a current Amiberry.

@endnode

@node HostCopy "host-copy"
host-copy copies one file between any host path and the Amiga. The host folder does not have to be mounted as an Amiga volume.

Usage:

host-copy get <host path> <Amiga destination> [FORCE]
host-copy put <Amiga source> <host path> [FORCE]

get copies a host file to the Amiga. put copies an Amiga file to the host. Only single files are copied, and a file must be smaller than 2 GB.

The host path is typed the way the host writes it, for example /home/me/notes.txt on Linux and macOS or C:\Users\me\notes.txt on Windows. A leading ~ stands for the host user's home folder. A relative path starts in the folder Amiberry was started from. On Linux and macOS the host path is converted from ISO-8859-1 to UTF-8, unless it is already valid UTF-8 (as AROS sends it).

The destination may be a folder, in which case the file keeps its name. An existing destination file is overwritten only when FORCE is given.

get writes a temporary file beside the Amiga destination, checks its size and moves it into place. put has the host write a temporary file named .htput.<number> beside the host target, check its size and move it into place. host-copy reports success only after the host confirms the size. Without FORCE, put never replaces a file that appears on the host during the upload. A failed or aborted copy leaves the old destination alone.

There is no time limit while the host process runs. Press Ctrl-C to abort. host-copy needs an Amiberry build with host pipe sessions. Linux and macOS hosts need sh, head, wc, cat, tr and mv, and Windows hosts need PowerShell.

@endnode

@node HostEnv "host-env"
host-env gets, sets, removes and lists host user environment variables.

Usage:

host-env get NAME
host-env set NAME VALUE
host-env unset NAME
host-env list

On Windows, host-env updates the user's persistent environment through PowerShell. New host processes see the changed values.

On Linux and macOS, set and unset change the file $HOME/.host-tools-env. The file is replaced atomically and restricted to the host user with mode 0600. Commands that run at the same time take turns, so no change is lost; one that cannot get its turn within five seconds fails with a message. Source it from your host shell startup files if you want future host login shells to import the values. get and list read the host session's environment and the values from that file.

host-env cannot change the live environment of host shells, desktop applications or Amiberry's parent process that are already running.

Variable names use ASCII letters, digits and underscores and must not start with a digit. Values must not contain line breaks.

@endnode

@node HostQuit "host-quit"
host-quit closes Amiberry, as quitting from its menu does. Amiberry's WHDLoad booter runs it when a game exits.

Usage:

host-quit

host-quit works on Linux, macOS and Windows hosts. It needs uae.resource but not Native Code. It waits until Amiberry closes; Ctrl-C returns.

@endnode

@node InstallNotes "Installation Notes"
The installer offers five components: command tools, this AmigaGuide, UAE AHI, UAESND AHI and the UAE MHI MP3 decoder. All are selected by default. Novice installs everything and replaces existing files. Intermediate lets you select components and copies files without asking, but leaves an installed program or library alone when it is the same version or newer. Expert lets you select components and asks before replacing files, showing installed and package versions when available.

The guide is installed to HELP:<language>/Host-Tools.guide (English if no language is set). The drivers go to DEVS:AHI/ and DEVS:AudioModes/, and the decoder goes to LIBS:MHI/. The installer does not edit startup files, system settings, AHI preferences or driver configuration.

Enable Native Code execution in Amiberry to use the command tools (except host-quit) and the MHI decoder. Enable the UAESND sound board in Amiberry before you use the UAESND AHI modes. Choose your AHI mode in AHI preferences yourself.

All command tools work on Linux, macOS and Windows hosts with a current Amiberry. The exception is host-shell on Windows, which needs Amiberry 8.6.0 or newer and Windows 10 version 1809 or later for the ConPTY pseudo console.

host-reveal, host-notify, host-clip, host-say, host-info and host-env can be cancelled with Ctrl-C, and so can host-run, host-multiview and host-edit on Windows. They give up when the host sends no output for about 30 seconds on Amiberry builds with status support, or 5 seconds on older builds. host-say is the exception on builds with status support: it waits until the speech ends.

@endnode
